Pin javac's --release to 17 (was hard-coded 21) so the M150 Android AAR's
classes.jar ships Java 17 class files (major version 61), matching M144 and
the app's own toolchain (target 17, JVM unit tests run on JDK 17 on the VPS
and the self-hosted CI - only JDK 17 is installed there).

Root cause: there is no GN arg for this. build/android/gyp/compile_java.py
hard-codes javac's -release flag:

  javac_args = [
      ...
      '--release',
      # Keep consistent with javac_cmd in //build/android/gyp/turbine.py.
      '21',
      ...

(compile_java.py:607-609), and build/android/gyp/turbine.py duplicates the
same literal for header-jar generation:

  javac_cmd = ['--release', '21']

(turbine.py:94, comment cross-references compile_java.py and
//build/android/gradle/android.jinja - the .jinja is Gradle-project-
generation only, not used by build_aar.py, so it is untouched here).
build/android/gyp/compile_kt.py already passes "-jvm-target" "11" to kotlinc
(compile_kt.py:150-151) - Kotlin output was never the problem, only javac's
--release 21 output was.

This is the `build` repo, a separate git checkout nested inside the webrtc
src tree at <src>/build (DEPS: 'src/build':
'https://github.com/webrtc-sdk/build@d2fe8a958bfecec159f7a478ba487e41dd635a13',
itself a GitHub mirror/fork of chromium/src/build - chromium.googlesource.com
does not carry this exact fork commit). apply-series.sh's resolve_dir() must
learn the 'build' -> "$SRC/build" mapping for this patch's series line to
resolve (companion change, not in this patch: m150/apply-series.sh).

Both hunks verified with `git apply --check -v` against a fresh sparse
shallow clone of https://github.com/webrtc-sdk/build.git @
d2fe8a958bfecec159f7a478ba487e41dd635a13 (the exact pin above).

Java-version safety: `--release 17` only lowers the class file's target/API
level; the JDK actually invoked to run javac/turbine is still whatever
third_party/jdk/current resolves to (DEPS pins a CIPD JDK 21 package for
linux-amd64), so no toolchain swap is needed - `javac --release 17` from a
JDK 21 javac is a normal, supported cross-compilation mode. Checked
sdk/android at webrtc-sdk/webrtc @ ba469aa2093ba950066258ca0a59a6fbd1295582
(235 .java/.kt files) for anything --release 17 would reject: no switch
pattern matching (`case Type v ->`/`case Type v:`), no record patterns, no
`record` class declarations, no `sealed`/`permits` keywords. Text blocks
(Java 15) and records (Java 16) would have been fine under 17 anyway; none
were found either.

diff --git a/android/gyp/compile_java.py b/android/gyp/compile_java.py
index 9892395..a65ad66 100644
--- a/android/gyp/compile_java.py
+++ b/android/gyp/compile_java.py
@@ -606,7 +606,7 @@ def main(argv,
       # Jacoco does not currently support a higher value.
       '--release',
       # Keep consistent with javac_cmd in //build/android/gyp/turbine.py.
-      '21',
+      '17',
       # Chromium only allows UTF8 source files.  Being explicit avoids
       # javac pulling a default encoding from the user's environment.
       '-encoding',
diff --git a/android/gyp/turbine.py b/android/gyp/turbine.py
index d719897..dab4517 100644
--- a/android/gyp/turbine.py
+++ b/android/gyp/turbine.py
@@ -91,7 +91,7 @@ def main(argv):
   # Keep consistent with javac_args release flag in
   # //build/android/gyp/compile_java.py and
   # compileOptions in //build/android/gradle/android.jinja
-  javac_cmd = ['--release', '21']
+  javac_cmd = ['--release', '17']

   # Turbine reads lists from command line args by consuming args until one
   # starts with double dash (--). Thus command line args should be grouped
